nodecode-doppler-geo is a rewrite of mojomast's mojomast/hermes-aircraft-doppler-geolocation:

  https://github.com/mojomast/hermes-aircraft-doppler-geolocation
  commit 14b4b0c841de0a77c0eb405a311acc540d3a1b58
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Kyle Durepos (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the two tools the plugin registered, doppler_detect and
doppler_locate, as an nle:define-cell with the same names and the same
arguments, a /doppler command and a manual topic. The audio path is plain
Common Lisp instead of numpy and ffmpeg: a RIFF/WAVE reader, an in-place
radix-2 FFT over a Hann-windowed spectrogram, one ridge per frame, and the
same straight-pass Doppler model fitted by a coarse grid over (speed, slant
distance, closest approach) with the rest tone solved in closed form, then
refined. The locator does the same thing in the same order: predict each
event's curve from real ADS-B tracks at every candidate cell, score the
least-squares misfit, turn it into a posterior, and report the best point,
the top five, the 68 and 95 per cent areas and a confidence flag.

Left out, and why: the ADS-B download (a day of adsb.lol history is about 3 GB
streamed per uncached day, which needs a streaming tar and gzip this image does
not carry; the cell reads the same columns from a CSV the user gives it), the
Copernicus DEM and Open-Meteo fetch (ground elevation is sea level here unless
the region is flat), the experimental broadband ground-reflection mode (a
cepstrogram and a Viterbi tracker of its own), the diagnostic PNGs, and
ffmpeg decoding (RIFF/WAVE only, so any other container is converted first).
